        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.478,
            0.847,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "While discussing solids that contain plane surfaces in their boundaries, the notion of faces, edges and vertices serves a useful purpose."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.226,
            0.528,
            0.749,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.673,
            0.759,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.823,
            0.848,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": "**Faces** are the plane/flat surfaces of a solid that form its boundary. **Edges** are the line segments that form the sides of the faces, and **vertices** are the points at which the edges meet. For example, a cuboid or cube has 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices."
        },
